      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To determine how many times Terrell must lift two 15-pound weights to lift the same total weight as he does with two 20-pound weights, we first need to calculate the total weight lifted with the 20-pound weights and then find out how many times lifting two 15-pound weights will achieve the same total weight.\n\n1. **Calculate the total weight lifted with the 20-pound weights:**\n\n   - Weight per weight: 20 pounds\n   - Number of weights lifted: 12 times\n   - Total weight lifted = Weight per weight * Number of weights lifted\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total weight lifted} = 20 \\, \\text{pounds} \\times 12 \\, \\text{times} = 240 \\, \\text{pounds}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the total weight lifted with the 15-pound weights:**\n\n   - Weight per weight: 15 pounds\n   - Number of weights lifted: 12 times (same as before)\n   - Total weight lifted = Weight per weight * Number of weights lifted\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total weight lifted} = 15 \\, \\text{pounds} \\times 12 \\, \\text{times} = 180 \\, \\text{pounds}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Find out how many times two 15-pound weights must be lifted to achieve the same total weight as with two 20-pound weights:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total weight lifted needed} = 240 \\, \\text{pounds}\n   \\]\n\n   - Total weight lifted with two 15-pound weights = 180 pounds\n   - Number of times 15-pound weights must be lifted: \n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of times} = \\frac{240 \\, \\text{pounds}}{15 \\, \\text{pounds/weight}} \\times 2 = \\frac{240}{15} \\times 2 = 16 \\times 2 = 24\n   \\]\n\nThus, Terrell must lift the two 15-pound weights \\(\\boxed{24}\\) times to lift the same total weight as he does with the 20-pound weights.",
